    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm working on a 12-week hypertrophy block for a client but they've completely stalled on their back squat. They've hit a plateau where they can't add any weight for three weeks straight. Can you explain the physiological reasoning behind using a deload week here? Also, how should I approach volume manipulation to help them break through this? Thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A deload week is physiologically necessary when accumulating high volumes to prevent excessive central nervous system fatigue and systemic inflammation that cause strength stagnation. During this recovery period, athletes can adequately tissue repair and replenish energy stores, often leading to a sudden surplus of available strength once training resumes. Regarding volume manipulation, consider shifting from a linear progression to undulating microcycles that insert lighter high-volume sets to enhance metabolic stress without adding mechanical tension. Pairing these modifications with freshness-enhancing stretches or mobility work will further support the central nervous system in breaking through the plateau.",
      "source": "target",
